{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2001 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5f8219d7483e8c980ade47530ba71c60ed6b9121a54ed3f4e01f4495bf4eb850" :edb/db-before-t 2000 :edb/db-after-t 2001 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535313 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:50:34.431Z" 13194139535313 true] [17592186068652 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343261 13194139535313 true] [17592186078307 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343239 13194139535313 true] [17592186083240 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343242 13194139535313 true] [17592186087828 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343251 13194139535313 true] [17592186096605 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343254 13194139535313 true] [17592186096729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343245 13194139535313 true] [17592186096729 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343248 13194139535313 true] [17592186096760 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343233 13194139535313 true] [17592186096763 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343236 13194139535313 true] [17592186184098 1020 #edb/ref 17592186343264 13194139535313 true] [17592186343233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343234 13194139535313 true] [17592186343233 1089 "q-132974" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343233 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535313 true] [17592186343233 1100 "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343233 1113 #uuid "6a320cd6-807e-5997-8b65-5fa33e189660" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343233 1145 "First, let's define a function $h(t):$\n\n$$\nh(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}\n$$\n\nTo differentiate this function, we use the chain rule. Let \n\n$$\ng(t)=2{t}^{2}+3,\\,h(g(t))=g(t)^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating our functions, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =(2{t}^{2}+3)^{′}=4t, \\\\ {h}^{′}(g(t)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}] & ={h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={h}^{′}(g(t))\\cdot {g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot 4t \\\\  & =8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343234 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343235 13194139535313 true] [17592186343234 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535313 true] [17592186343234 1041 "field-1" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343234 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343235 13194139535313 true] [17592186343234 1110 #uuid "d7881a1e-264b-54f5-92b8-6669fea0f773" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343235 1034 "16t^3+24t" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343235 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535313 true] [17592186343235 1111 #uuid "1593f063-3246-54e5-8621-ba9abad3c944" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343236 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343237 13194139535313 true] [17592186343236 1089 "q-132975" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343236 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535313 true] [17592186343236 1100 "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343236 1113 #uuid "99412c9e-d222-55f7-9078-6dcf7bb7d499" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343236 1145 "To differentiate the function\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwe use the quotient rule with the numerator $g(t)=2t$ and the denominator $h(t)=(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}.$\n\nDifferentiating $g(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=2,\n$$\n\nand from part 1, we have\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(t)=8t(2{t}^{2}+3).\n$$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{{g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)-g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t)}{[h(t)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2)\\cdot (2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-(2t)\\cdot (8t(2{t}^{2}+3))}{{((2{t}^{2}+3)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}-16{t}^{2}(2{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{t}^{2}+6-16{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343237 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343238 13194139535313 true] [17592186343237 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535313 true] [17592186343237 1041 "field-1" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343237 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343238 13194139535313 true] [17592186343237 1110 #uuid "d5506a20-9f8a-52fe-abfc-e942cce02522" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343238 1034 "\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343238 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535313 true] [17592186343238 1111 #uuid "f468ae22-6b17-56e6-b319-588500df770a" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343239 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343240 13194139535313 true] [17592186343239 1089 "q-132976" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343239 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535313 true] [17592186343239 1100 "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343239 1113 #uuid "9f987e35-1a6f-5179-a128-776cd6f81c42" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343239 1145 "The instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nIn part 2, we found that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{6-12(1)^{2}}{(2(1)^{2}+3)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is $-\\frac{6}{125}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343240 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343241 13194139535313 true] [17592186343240 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535313 true] [17592186343240 1041 "field-1" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343240 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343241 13194139535313 true] [17592186343240 1110 #uuid "f3b94854-0842-5e48-8133-ccbf5875df06" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343241 1034 "-\\frac{6}{125}" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343241 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535313 true] [17592186343241 1111 #uuid "e4c276cc-aeed-5362-acbf-ff4efe755cde" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343242 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343243 13194139535313 true] [17592186343242 1089 "q-132977" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343242 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535313 true] [17592186343242 1100 "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds." 13194139535313 true] [17592186343242 1113 #uuid "96f735fc-ef04-5f32-b866-b00213d2686a" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343242 1145 "A critical point of $x(t)$ is a point $t=c$ in the domain of $x$ such that either ${x}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${x}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nIn part 2, we obtained\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${x}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 6-12{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 6 & =12{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $x(t)$ is only defined for $t>0.$ Therefore, $x(t)$ has a stationary point at $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n* Second, we note that ${x}^{′}(t)$ exists for all values of $t$ in the domain of $x(t).$ So $x(t)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical point of $x(t)$ is $t=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343243 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343244 13194139535313 true] [17592186343243 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535313 true] [17592186343243 1041 "field-1" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343243 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343244 13194139535313 true] [17592186343243 1110 #uuid "885f525a-e981-55f4-ba11-5ea31b1e9516" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343244 1034 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343244 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535313 true] [17592186343244 1111 #uuid "fc4033c6-2524-5144-a1be-2381e92ae647" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343245 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343246 13194139535313 true] [17592186343245 1089 "q-132978" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343245 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535313 true] [17592186343245 1100 "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{6-12t^2}{(2t^2+3)^3}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nThe instantaneous rate of change in the particle's $x$-value when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{6}{125}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nIt can be shown that the particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction at the critical points of $x(t).$ Find these critical points.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The particle comes to an instantaneous rest in the $x$-direction when $t=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         seconds.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343245 1113 #uuid "0e21b36e-0c82-5fdb-add8-811f9e999707" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343245 1145 "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot 2{(\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343246 1000 #edb/ref 17592186343247 13194139535313 true] [17592186343246 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535313 true] [17592186343246 1041 "field-1" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343246 1104 #edb/ref 17592186343247 13194139535313 true] [17592186343246 1110 #uuid "517735cb-10ed-594d-822e-271805774976" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343247 1034 "\\frac{6-12t^2}{t(2t^2+3)}" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343247 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535313 true] [17592186343247 1111 #uuid "b93f04b3-4ce2-5194-99b0-d9bd09680c25" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343248 1017 #edb/ref 17592186343249 13194139535313 true] [17592186343248 1089 "q-132979" 13194139535313 true] [17592186343248 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535313 true] [17592186343248 1100 "A particle $P$ is moving along a curve in an $xy$-plane such that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,t>0\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the distance of $P$ from the origin, measured in meters, and $t$ is measured in seconds.\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}]=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $16t^3+24t$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${x}^{′}(t)=\\,$\n\n   